  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Northern Metalmark is a small butterfly belonging to the Riodinidae family, characterized by its metallic-looking wings with intricate patterns. It is one of the few metalmark species found in New England and represents the northern range limit of this primarily tropical family.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Metallic Spots and Bands",
      "description": "Look for distinctive silvery or metallic-colored spots and bands on the wings, which give the family its common name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "Adults are very small butterflies with a wingspan of only 0.8 to 1.1 inches, making them easy to overlook",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Orange-Red Coloring",
      "description": "Wings display reddish-orange coloring with darker markings and metallic patches, especially visible on the hindwings",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Northern Metalmarks inhabit open woodlands, forest edges, and areas with scrubby vegetation in New England, particularly where their host plants like dock and sorrel species are present.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite their delicate appearance, Northern Metalmarks have an unusual behavior where they rest with their wings folded vertically over their backs rather than horizontally, making them cryptic and difficult to spot in their natural habitat.",
